{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,160p' paper.tex; cat references.bib; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,graphicx,booktabs,subcaption,longtable}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Fixed-Game SHAP Reliability:\\\\Structure, Budgets, and Reference Limits}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\norm}[1]{\\left\\lVert #1\\right\\rVert_1}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nA feature attribution can be repeatable without accurately computing its intended explanation, and accurate without justifying a decision. We isolate the computational question for frozen tabular classifiers: agreement with a fixed empirical, probability-scale Shapley game under finite coalition caps. We compare library Kernel SHAP, averaging two split-cap estimates, and structure-aware computation using forest leaf constraints and logistic integration. German Credit and an explicitly adapted ACS income task provide logistic and forest conditions with different reference quality. Across the retained final evaluation, structure-aware outputs have mean normalized reference agreement of 0.99756 and identical observed attribution vectors across repeated calls; their top-three sets agree with every reference. However, German references are approximate, and the structure-aware methods access model parameters rather than spending the same prediction-only budget. Split-cap averaging is particularly weak at the smallest German cap. These results support a narrow computational-reliability claim for the evaluated fixed games, not equal-time superiority, robustness to background changes, or reliable reasons for high-stakes decisions.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/reference_fidelity]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/max_repeat_vector_difference]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nFeature attributions are often presented as a compact rationale for an individual prediction. The SHAP framework assigns additive feature contributions using a Shapley-value construction \\citep{Lundberg2017,Shapley1953}. Computing these contributions can require approximation, so an explanation obtained under a practical budget should be assessed as a numerical result, not accepted merely because the attribution interface returns a vector. This matters particularly when a small set of highly ranked features becomes the public-facing account of a credit or income prediction.\n\nThere are several distinct reliability questions. Does repeating the same calculation change the answer? Does the returned vector approximate the intended Shapley values? Is the underlying explanation game appropriate for the application? Does that game provide a justified reason for acting on the prediction? The first two are computational questions; they do not settle the latter two. Explanation disagreement across methods \\citep{Krishna2022}, dependence on background data \\citep{Yuan2022}, and the distinction between observational and interventional games \\citep{Chen2020} make this separation essential.\n\nWe study a deliberately restricted setting: a frozen classifier, fixed query, fixed finite background, and a probability output. The central question is whether one returned attribution vector is dependable as a computation of that \\emph{particular} game. Our hypothesis is that exploiting an exposed classifier's structure can remove substantial sampling error, but that repeatability alone remains insufficient evidence of attribution accuracy. The comparison is between computational access regimes, not just interchangeable random seeds: prediction-only estimators sample hybrid inputs, whereas the structure-aware implementation also uses tree arrays or logistic coefficients.\n\nThe study combines a held-out evaluation with matched development comparisons. The former supplies the principal measurements; the latter helps localize changes to the corresponding model family without presenting development selection as independent confirmation. Across the final German Credit and ACS conditions, observed repeated-call vectors are identical and normalized reference agreement is high. Crucially, exhaustive ACS references and approximate German references allow different strengths of interpretation. We retain this asymmetry rather than reporting every comparison as distance to ground truth.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nOur contributions are: (i) a fixed-game protocol that separately measures reference distance, top-feature membership, sign agreement, repeat consistency, and endpoint conservation; (ii) a concrete, inspectable comparison of sampled estimates with forest leaf-game and probability-scale logistic computations; and (iii) evidence about the limits of those measurements, including split-budget failure, approximate-reference disagreement, and unequal computational access. We do not claim a new general theory of explanation validity or a new invention of exact tree attribution. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the evidence structure.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fixed_game_overview.png}\n\\caption{Computational reliability for a fixed explanation game. Sampled and structure-aware calculations target the same finite-background probability game. Repeat consistency and reference agreement answer different questions; neither establishes deployment validity.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Attribution targets and computation.}\nShapley values originate in cooperative games \\citep{Shapley1953}; SHAP connects this allocation principle to additive prediction explanations \\citep{Lundberg2017}. Multilinear game extensions \\citep{Owen1972} motivate integrating expected marginal contributions over inclusion probabilities. Sampling-based Shapley calculation \\citep{Castro2009} and leverage-score sampling \\citep{Musco2024} address computational cost without requiring tree-specific structure. Exact tree attribution is established prior work \\citep{Lundberg2018}. Our contribution is the bounded empirical comparison and its interpretive limits, not priority for exact forest explanations. Leverage SHAP and a separate TreeSHAP implementation were not evaluated here.\n\n\\paragraph{Different meanings of stability.}\nPractitioner-facing disagreement between explanation methods \\citep{Krishna2022} differs from repeating a computation with the game fixed. Background-size effects in deep models \\citep{Yuan2022} also change a source of variation that we hold constant. Interventional and observational expectations can answer different questions \\citep{Chen2020}; accurate computation does not choose between them. Predictive multiplicity \\citep{Marx2020} concerns competing trained models, whereas our primary repeat comparison freezes each fitted model. Feature-selection stability \\citep{Nogueira2018} and rank-biased overlap \\citep{Webber2010} provide context for comparing feature sets and rankings. Our reported top-three intersection is a simple membership measure, not either of those specialized statistics.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty and scope.}\nBounds for independent bounded observations \\citep{Hoeffding1963} and variance-sensitive empirical bounds \\citep{Maurer2009} motivate internal safeguards in the sampled fallback. Their presence does not establish observed acceptance coverage. Bootstrap resampling \\citep{Efron1979} supports conditional uncertainty over query instances, not an extrapolation over new populations. ACS-based tasks \\citep{Ding2021} provide a public income setting; German Credit supplies a smaller credit setting \\citep{GermanCredit}. Logistic regression \\citep{Hosmer2013} and random forests \\citep{Breiman2001} expose structures useful for this computational comparison. Tabular neural architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021} lie outside its measured model coverage.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Fixed-Game Target and Reliability Criteria}\nLet $f:\\mathbb{R}^d\\rightarrow[0,1]$ be a frozen classifier's positive-class probability, $x$ a query, and $Z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_B)$ a fixed background. A coalition $S\\subseteq D=\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ selects query coordinates and replaces all others with coordinates from one background row:\n\\begin{align}\n v_x(S)&=\\frac{1}{B}\\sum_{b=1}^B f(x_S,z_{b,D\\setminus S}), \\\\\n \\phi_j&=\\sum_{S\\subseteq D\\setminus\\{j\\}}\\frac{|S|!\\,(d-|S|-1)!}{d!}\n       \\big[v_x(S\\cup\\{j\\})-v_x(S)\\big]. \\label{eq:shapley}\n\\end{align}\nThis is an empirical replacement game, not a conditional-distribution estimator. Each supplied scalar-encoded original feature is a player. The probability scale and background remain identical across compared computations. In particular, logistic coefficients on the log-odds scale are not substituted for probability-scale attributions.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L11-L64]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L757-L796]]\n\nFor an estimate $\\widehat\\phi$ and recorded reference $\\phi^{\\rm ref}$, we use normalized L1 reference agreement\n\\begin{equation}\n A(\\widehat\\phi,\\phi^{\\rm ref})=\n \\left(1+\\frac{\\norm{\\widehat\\phi-\\phi^{\\rm ref}}}\n {\\max(\\norm{\\phi^{\\rm ref}},10^{-3})}\\right)^{-1}. \\label{eq:agreement}\n\\end{equation}\nThe floor prevents division by a near-zero reference norm; the score is not a probability of explanation validity. Let $T(a)$ be the indices of the three largest $|a_j|$, with original coordinate order resolving ties. Reference top-three overlap is $|T(\\widehat\\phi)\\cap T(\\phi^{\\rm ref})|/\\min(3,d)$. Sign agreement is the fraction of coordinates with equal signs, treating magnitudes at most $10^{-8}$ as zero. We also report endpoint residual\n\\begin{equation}\n E=\\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac{|\\sum_j\\widehat\\phi_j-[f(x)-v_x(\\varnothing)]|}\n {\\max(|f(x)-v_x(\\varnothing)|,10^{-3})}\\right).\n\\end{equation}\nFor each pair of repeated calls, we compute the same top-three and sign agreements between their outputs rather than against a reference. These pairwise summaries are kept separate from reference agreement throughout the paper. Endpoint conservation is a consistency check, not proof of correct feature allocation.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1014-L1030]]\n\n\\subsection{Compared Computations}\n\\paragraph{Prediction-only baselines.}\nThe library baseline uses \\texttt{shap.KernelExplainer} with the identity link, no L1 selection penalty, and the requested coalition cap $C$. Its split-cap comparator averages independent estimates using $\\lfloor C/2\\rfloor$ samples each. Both use the same serialized probability predictor, background, and queries as the structure-aware implementation. This comparator evaluates budget splitting, not the effect of doubling total resources. Endpoints, background expansion, and setup costs mean a coalition cap is not a literal wall-time or total prediction-row equality.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L757-L772]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L848-L871]]\n\n\\paragraph{Forest leaf-game computation.}\nFor every tree, the implementation intersects all splits on a feature along a root-to-leaf path. With query $x$ and background row $z_b$ fixed, a feature can satisfy the leaf constraint only when included, only when excluded, in both states, or in neither. A neither-state feature makes that leaf's game identically zero. Otherwise write $P$ for required inclusions and $Q$ for required exclusions, with sizes $p,q$; both-state features are dummy players. The leaf indicator is\n\\begin{equation}\n g(S)=\\mathbf{1}\\{P\\subseteq S,\\;Q\\cap S=\\varnothing\\}.\n\\end{equation}\nIntegrating the inclusion-probability marginal gives contribution coefficients\n\\begin{equation}\n \\phi_j(g)=\\begin{cases}\n   [p\\binom{p+q}{p}]^{-1},&j\\in P,\\\\\n   -[q\\binom{p+q}{p}]^{-1},&j\\in Q,\\\\\n   0,&j\\notin P\\cup Q.\n \\end{cases} \\label{eq:leaf}\n\\end{equation}\nMultiplying by leaf probabilities and averaging over trees and background rows exploits Shapley linearity. This avoids sampled coalition predictions for the forest branch. The implementation respects repeated-feature splits and the supplied predictor's float32 input and float64 threshold semantics; exactness refers to this finite, serialized game, up to floating-point arithmetic.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/forest.py#L11-L81]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L720-L755]]\n\n\\paragraph{Probability-scale logistic integration.}\nFor one background row define $a=\\beta_0+\\beta^\\top z_b$ and $u_j=\\beta_j(x_j-z_{bj})$. The coalition probability is $\\sigma(a+\\sum_{j\\in S}u_j)$. The implemented sigmoid identity and inclusion integral yield\n\\begin{align}\n \\sigma(s)&=\\tfrac12+\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{\\sin(\\omega s)}{\\sinh(\\pi\\omega)}\\,d\\omega,\\\\\n \\phi_j^{(b)}&=\\int_0^\\infty\\!\\frac{\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\left[e^{i\\omega a}(e^{i\\omega u_j}-1)\n \\int_0^1\\prod_{k\\ne j}(1-t+t e^{i\\omega u_k})\\,dt\\right]}\n {\\sinh(\\pi\\omega)}\\,d\\omega. \\label{eq:logistic}\n\\end{align}\nThe inner polynomial has degree at most $d-1$, so $\\lceil d/2\\rceil$ Gauss--Legendre nodes integrate it exactly in exact arithmetic. Prefix/suffix products avoid division by possibly zero factors. The outer frequency integral is numerical, not an exact logistic SHAP solver.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L12-L40]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L97-L140]]\n\nThe implementation truncates frequencies at 8, uses eight-node quadrature panels, and refines up to 2048 frequency nodes. It requires two consecutive mesh agreements and an endpoint residual within $10^{-8}$, with an analytic tail tolerance of $10^{-10}$. Mesh agreement does not bound every coordinate's discretization error. If any background row fails numerical checks, the whole logistic integration is rejected and the sampled fallback is retained. All supplied background rows are averaged; these checks do not select a favorable subset.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L43-L94]]\n\n\\paragraph{Dispatch and sampled fallback.}\nForest structure is used directly. Other games are enumerated when the cap covers their nontrivial coalitions; otherwise logistic integration is considered. The fallback combines independent complete permutation paths with complement-paired kernel-distributed masks and an importance-corrected, endpoint-constrained regression. An internal signed top-feature separation test may select the path estimate instead. We have no recorded dispatch frequencies or test-acceptance coverage and make no empirical certification claim. The source ordering matters: high-cap exhaustive behavior must not be attributed to quadrature alone.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L163-L197]]\n\n\\subsection{Design Rationale and Component Comparisons}\nThe design removes sampling where model structure permits same-game algebra, rather than changing what absent features mean. Forest leaf constraints expose a finite conjunction game; logistic additivity in the logit allows a probability-scale transform. We therefore expect the largest benefits where prediction-only estimates are inaccurate under the supplied cap. Deterministic computation is not itself the hypothesis test: an incorrect deterministic vector would be perfectly repeatable.\n\nTwo matched development comparisons examine the forest component with the nonforest computation retained, and logistic integration with the forest component retained. Readable source differences support this limited interpretation. These are narrower comparisons than a claim that every observed configuration difference isolates a single mathematical choice: the components also change access to model structure and computational work. There is no factorial study of quadrature safeguards, background size, or permutation-test acceptance.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/sampled/reliability.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/forest/reliability.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py]]\n\n\\section{Experimental Setup}\n\\paragraph{Data and frozen models.}\nThe final evaluation uses German Credit and ACSIncome-2024-RELSHIPP, each with logistic regression and a random forest. German retains 300 training, 100 validation, and 100 explanation records with 20 feature groups. ACS retains 20000, 6667, and 6667 records with 10 groups. Only the first 16 explanation records and first 16 training rows are used as queries and background in each condition; these are fixed across repeated calls, not randomly redrawn per explanation. Thus the split sizes should not be mistaken for the number of explained individuals.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L687-L718]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py#L61-L155]]\n\nGerman records are partitioned by normalized predictor content into disjoint development and final subsets. ACS uses California for development and New York for final evaluation, after excluding cross-state predictor-content overlaps. The final ACS task replaces the historical \\texttt{RELP} field, absent from the retained release schema, with native \\texttt{RELSHIPP}; it is not bit-identical to historical ACSIncome. Other predictors, the adult population filter, and the income threshold are retained in the acquisition code. These choices define the evaluated population, not a population-representative survey analysis.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/data_provenance.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py#L87-L155]]\n\nCategorical vocabularies are fitted on training data and encoded ordinally; numerical missing values use training medians. Training means and standard deviations standardize all supplied groups. No one-hot masking study is included. Logistic models use \\texttt{lbfgs}, a 500-iteration cap and tolerance $10^{-7}$. Forests use 64 trees, maximum depth 6, and a single training thread. Model parameters and preprocessing remain frozen within a repeat comparison. Ordinal categories can impose artificial order; our results concern these fitted predictors, not optimal credit or income models.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L687-L718]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L817-L848]]\n\n\\paragraph{Caps, seeds, and references.}\nFinal caps are 64, 256, and 1024. Each condition has six repeated structure-aware calls per cap for seeds 104729, 130363, and 155921. Each seed produces a fitted model/reference evaluation; queries and backgrounds are reused. ACS references enumerate all coalitions of the ten-feature game. German references average two independent library estimates, each with cap 4096. Their coordinatewise half-difference is retained as a reference-disagreement proxy, not a confidence interval or an error bound for their mean.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L848-L858]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1314-L1320]]\n\n\\paragraph{Reporting and uncertainty.}\nWe average metrics over query instances within each condition, then over the recorded seeds with equal weights. Repeated calls on the same query are not independent individuals. The stored 95\\% intervals resample the 16 query-level mean agreements using 1000 bootstrap resamples, after averaging caps and repeats within each fitted condition. They are neither pairwise-method confidence intervals nor uncertainty over retraining, background selection, or future deployment. Main plots show seed-level points and means, not invented standard errors. Library comparators have one recorded estimate per seed and cap; their within-model repeated-call consistency was not measured by this final protocol.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1154-L1159]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThe completed final evaluation met its documented qualification checks. This establishes coverage and execution/control requirements, not semantic validity or a performance-based deployment threshold. No missing or failed empirical cell is used as evidence of inferiority. The full evaluation workload took 700.37 seconds of wall time, including references and comparators; candidate-only latency and total CPU-hours are unavailable. Development comparisons use two seeds, smaller query sets, and caps 64 and 256 and are reported separately.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/wall_seconds]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Main Fixed-Game Comparison}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:final} compares computations within each dataset/model condition. At cap 64, mean structure-aware agreement is 0.9966 for German logistic and 0.9937 for German forest, against 0.9347 and 0.8806 for the one-shot library baseline. On ACS, both structure-aware conditions round to 1.0000 against the exhaustive reference. The corresponding one-shot library means are 0.9577 and 0.9636. These comparisons support improved numerical agreement in the retained low-cap settings, not a claim about new people, backgrounds, or classifiers.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n@misc{Lundberg2017,\n  title = {{A Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions}},\n  author = {Scott Lundberg and Su-In Lee},\n  year = {2017},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1705.07874},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.1705.07874}\n}\n\n@misc{Krishna2022,\n  title = {{The Disagreement Problem in Explainable Machine Learning: A Practitioner's Perspective}},\n  author = {Satyapriya Krishna and Tessa Han and Alex Gu and Shahin Jabbari and Zhiwei Steven Wu and Himabindu Lakkaraju},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.01602},\n  doi = {10.21203/rs.3.rs-2963888/v1}\n}\n\n@misc{Yuan2022,\n  title = {{An empirical study of the effect of background data size on the stability of SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) for deep learning models}},\n  author = {Han Yuan and Mingxuan Liu and Lican Kang and Chenkui Miao and Ying Nian Wu and et al and Michael O. Krauthammer},\n  year = {2022},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2204.11351},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2204.11351}\n}\n\n@misc{Musco2024,\n  title = {{Provably Accurate Shapley Value Estimation via Leverage Score Sampling}},\n  author = {Christopher Musco and R. Teal Witter},\n  year = {2024},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.01917},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2410.01917}\n}\n\n@misc{Chen2020,\n  title = {{True to the Model or True to the Data?}},\n  author = {Hugh Chen and Joseph D. Janizek and Scott Lundberg and Su-In Lee},\n  year = {2020},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.16234},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2006.16234}\n}\n\n@misc{Marx2020,\n  title = {{Predictive Multiplicity in Classification}},\n  author = {Charles T. Marx and Flavio P. Calmon and Berk Ustun},\n  year = {2019},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1909.06677},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.1909.06677}\n}\n\n@misc{Nogueira2018,\n  title = {{On the stability of feature selection algorithms}},\n  author = {Sarah Nogueira and Konstantinos Sechidis and Gavin Brown B},\n  year = {2018},\n  howpublished = {Journal of Machine Learning Research, 18(174):1--54},\n  doi = {10.5555/3122009.3242031}\n}\n\n@misc{Webber2010,\n  title = {{A similarity measure for indefinite rankings}},\n  author = {William Webber and Alistair Moffat and Justin Zobel},\n  year = {2010},\n  howpublished = {ACM Transactions on Information Systems},\n  doi = {10.1145/1852102.1852106}\n}\n\n@misc{Ding2021,\n  title = {{Retiring Adult: New Datasets for Fair Machine Learning}},\n  author = {Frances Ding and Moritz Hardt and John Arthur Miller and Ludwig Schmidt},\n  year = {2021},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2108.04884},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2108.04884}\n}\n\n@misc{Gorishniy2021,\n  title = {{Revisiting Deep Learning Models for Tabular Data}},\n  author = {Yury Gorishniy and Ivan Rubachev and Valentin Khrulkov and Artem Babenko},\n  year = {2021},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.11959},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2106.11959}\n}\n\n@misc{Lundberg2018,\n  title = {{Consistent Individualized Feature Attribution for Tree Ensembles}},\n  author = {Scott Lundberg and Gabriel Erion and Su-In Lee},\n  year = {2018},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1802.03888},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.1802.03888}\n}\n\n@misc{Owen1972,\n  title = {{Multilinear Extensions of Games}},\n  author = {Guillermo Owen},\n  year = {1972},\n  howpublished = {Management Science},\n  doi = {10.1287/mnsc.18.5.64}\n}\n\n@misc{Shapley1953,\n  title = {{17. A Value for n-Person Games}},\n  author = {Lloyd S. Shapley},\n  year = {1953},\n  howpublished = {Princeton University Press eBooks},\n  doi = {10.1515/9781400881970-018}\n}\n\n@misc{Castro2009,\n  title = {{Polynomial calculation of the Shapley value based on sampling}},\n  author = {Javier Castro and Daniel Gomez and Juan Tejada},\n  year = {2008},\n  howpublished = {Computers \\& Operations Research},\n  doi = {10.1016/j.cor.2008.04.004}\n}\n\n@misc{Hoeffding1963,\n  title = {{Probability Inequalities for Sums of Bounded Random Variables}},\n  author = {Wassily Hoeffding},\n  year = {1963},\n  howpublished = {Journal of the American Statistical Association},\n  doi = {10.1080/01621459.1963.10500830}\n}\n\n@misc{Maurer2009,\n  title = {{Empirical Bernstein Bounds and Sample Variance Penalization}},\n  author = {Maurer, Andreas and Massimiliano Pontil},\n  year = {2009},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:0907.3740},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.0907.3740}\n}\n\n@misc{Efron1979,\n  title = {{Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife}},\n  author = {Bradley Efron},\n  year = {1979},\n  howpublished = {The Annals of Statistics},\n  doi = {10.1214/aos/1176344552}\n}\n\n@misc{Breiman2001,\n  title = {{Random Forests}},\n  author = {Leo Breiman},\n  year = {2001},\n  howpublished = {Machine Learning},\n  doi = {10.1023/a:1010933404324}\n}\n\n@misc{Hosmer2013,\n  title = {{Applied Logistic Regression}},\n  author = {David W. Hosmer and Stanley A. Lemeshow and Rodney X. Sturdivant},\n  year = {2013},\n  howpublished = {Wiley series in probability and statistics},\n  doi = {10.1002/9781118548387}\n}\n\n@misc{Pedregosa2011,\n  title = {{Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python}},\n  author = {PedregosaFabian and VaroquauxGael and GramfortAlexandre and MichelVincent and ThirionBertrand and GriselOlivier and BlondelMathieu and PrettenhoferPeter and WeissRon and DubourgVincent and VanderplasJake and PassosAlexandre and CournapeauDavid and BrucherMatthieu and PerrotMatthieu and DuchesnayEdouard},\n  year = {2011},\n  howpublished = {Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n  doi = {10.5555/1953048.2078195}\n}\n\n@misc{Harris2020,\n  title = {{Array programming with NumPy}},\n  author = {Charles R. Harris and K. Jarrod Millman and Stefan J. van der Walt and Ralf Gommers and Pauli Virtanen and David Cournapeau and Eric Wieser and Julian Taylor and Sebastian Berg and Nathaniel J. Smith and Robert Kern and Matti Picus and Stephan Hoyer and M. H. van Kerkwijk and Matthew Brett and Allan Haldane and Jaime Fernandez del Rio and Mark W