{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '35,145p'\"","aggregated_output":"    35\t\\end{figure}\n    36\t\n    37\t\\section{Related Work}\n    38\t\\paragraph{Attribution targets and computation.}\n    39\tShapley values originate in cooperative games \\citep{Shapley1953}; SHAP connects this allocation principle to additive prediction explanations \\citep{Lundberg2017}. Multilinear game extensions \\citep{Owen1972} motivate integrating expected marginal contributions over inclusion probabilities. Sampling-based Shapley calculation \\citep{Castro2009} and leverage-score sampling \\citep{Musco2024} address computational cost without requiring tree-specific structure. Exact tree attribution is established prior work \\citep{Lundberg2018}. Our contribution is the bounded empirical comparison and its interpretive limits, not priority for exact forest explanations. Leverage SHAP and a separate TreeSHAP implementation were not evaluated here.\n    40\t\n    41\t\\paragraph{Different meanings of stability.}\n    42\tPractitioner-facing disagreement between explanation methods \\citep{Krishna2022} differs from repeating a computation with the game fixed. Background-size effects in deep models \\citep{Yuan2022} also change a source of variation that we hold constant. Interventional and observational expectations can answer different questions \\citep{Chen2020}; accurate computation does not choose between them. Predictive multiplicity \\citep{Marx2020} concerns competing trained models, whereas our primary repeat comparison freezes each fitted model. Feature-selection stability \\citep{Nogueira2018} and rank-biased overlap \\citep{Webber2010} provide context for comparing feature sets and rankings. Our reported top-three intersection is a simple membership measure, not either of those specialized statistics.\n    43\t\n    44\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty and scope.}\n    45\tBounds for independent bounded observations \\citep{Hoeffding1963} and variance-sensitive empirical bounds \\citep{Maurer2009} motivate internal safeguards in the sampled fallback. Their presence does not establish observed acceptance coverage. Bootstrap resampling \\citep{Efron1979} supports conditional uncertainty over query instances, not an extrapolation over new populations. ACS-based tasks \\citep{Ding2021} provide a public income setting; German Credit supplies a smaller credit setting \\citep{GermanCredit}. Logistic regression \\citep{Hosmer2013} and random forests \\citep{Breiman2001} expose structures useful for this computational comparison. Tabular neural architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021} lie outside its measured model coverage.\n    46\t\n    47\t\\section{Method}\n    48\t\\subsection{Fixed-Game Target and Reliability Criteria}\n    49\tLet $f:\\mathbb{R}^d\\rightarrow[0,1]$ be a frozen classifier's positive-class probability, $x$ a query, and $Z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_B)$ a fixed background. A coalition $S\\subseteq D=\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ selects query coordinates and replaces all others with coordinates from one background row:\n    50\t\\begin{align}\n    51\t v_x(S)&=\\frac{1}{B}\\sum_{b=1}^B f(x_S,z_{b,D\\setminus S}), \\\\\n    52\t \\phi_j&=\\sum_{S\\subseteq D\\setminus\\{j\\}}\\frac{|S|!\\,(d-|S|-1)!}{d!}\n    53\t       \\big[v_x(S\\cup\\{j\\})-v_x(S)\\big]. \\label{eq:shapley}\n    54\t\\end{align}\n    55\tThis is an empirical replacement game, not a conditional-distribution estimator. Each supplied scalar-encoded original feature is a player. The probability scale and background remain identical across compared computations. In particular, logistic coefficients on the log-odds scale are not substituted for probability-scale attributions.\n    56\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L11-L64]]\n    57\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L757-L796]]\n    58\t\n    59\tFor an estimate $\\widehat\\phi$ and recorded reference $\\phi^{\\rm ref}$, we use normalized L1 reference agreement\n    60\t\\begin{equation}\n    61\t A(\\widehat\\phi,\\phi^{\\rm ref})=\n    62\t \\left(1+\\frac{\\norm{\\widehat\\phi-\\phi^{\\rm ref}}}\n    63\t {\\max(\\norm{\\phi^{\\rm ref}},10^{-3})}\\right)^{-1}. \\label{eq:agreement}\n    64\t\\end{equation}\n    65\tThe floor prevents division by a near-zero reference norm; the score is not a probability of explanation validity. Let $T(a)$ be the indices of the three largest $|a_j|$, with original coordinate order resolving ties. Reference top-three overlap is $|T(\\widehat\\phi)\\cap T(\\phi^{\\rm ref})|/\\min(3,d)$. Sign agreement is the fraction of coordinates with equal signs, treating magnitudes at most $10^{-8}$ as zero. We also report endpoint residual\n    66\t\\begin{equation}\n    67\t E=\\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac{|\\sum_j\\widehat\\phi_j-[f(x)-v_x(\\varnothing)]|}\n    68\t {\\max(|f(x)-v_x(\\varnothing)|,10^{-3})}\\right).\n    69\t\\end{equation}\n    70\tFor each pair of repeated calls, we compute the same top-three and sign agreements between their outputs rather than against a reference. These pairwise summaries are kept separate from reference agreement throughout the paper. Endpoint conservation is a consistency check, not proof of correct feature allocation.\n    71\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1014-L1030]]\n    72\t\n    73\t\\subsection{Compared Computations}\n    74\t\\paragraph{Prediction-only baselines.}\n    75\tThe library baseline uses \\texttt{shap.KernelExplainer} with the identity link, no L1 selection penalty, and the requested coalition cap $C$. Its split-cap comparator averages independent estimates using $\\lfloor C/2\\rfloor$ samples each. Both use the same serialized probability predictor, background, and queries as the structure-aware implementation. This comparator evaluates budget splitting, not the effect of doubling total resources. Endpoints, background expansion, and setup costs mean a coalition cap is not a literal wall-time or total prediction-row equality.\n    76\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L757-L772]]\n    77\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L848-L871]]\n    78\t\n    79\t\\paragraph{Forest leaf-game computation.}\n    80\tFor every tree, the implementation intersects all splits on a feature along a root-to-leaf path. With query $x$ and background row $z_b$ fixed, a feature can satisfy the leaf constraint only when included, only when excluded, in both states, or in neither. A neither-state feature makes that leaf's game identically zero. Otherwise write $P$ for required inclusions and $Q$ for required exclusions, with sizes $p,q$; both-state features are dummy players. The leaf indicator is\n    81\t\\begin{equation}\n    82\t g(S)=\\mathbf{1}\\{P\\subseteq S,\\;Q\\cap S=\\varnothing\\}.\n    83\t\\end{equation}\n    84\tIntegrating the inclusion-probability marginal gives contribution coefficients\n    85\t\\begin{equation}\n    86\t \\phi_j(g)=\\begin{cases}\n    87\t   [p\\binom{p+q}{p}]^{-1},&j\\in P,\\\\\n    88\t   -[q\\binom{p+q}{p}]^{-1},&j\\in Q,\\\\\n    89\t   0,&j\\notin P\\cup Q.\n    90\t \\end{cases} \\label{eq:leaf}\n    91\t\\end{equation}\n    92\tMultiplying by leaf probabilities and averaging over trees and background rows exploits Shapley linearity. This avoids sampled coalition predictions for the forest branch. The implementation respects repeated-feature splits and the supplied predictor's float32 input and float64 threshold semantics; exactness refers to this finite, serialized game, up to floating-point arithmetic.\n    93\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/forest.py#L11-L81]]\n    94\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L720-L755]]\n    95\t\n    96\t\\paragraph{Probability-scale logistic integration.}\n    97\tFor one background row define $a=\\beta_0+\\beta^\\top z_b$ and $u_j=\\beta_j(x_j-z_{bj})$. The coalition probability is $\\sigma(a+\\sum_{j\\in S}u_j)$. The implemented sigmoid identity and inclusion integral yield\n    98\t\\begin{align}\n    99\t \\sigma(s)&=\\tfrac12+\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{\\sin(\\omega s)}{\\sinh(\\pi\\omega)}\\,d\\omega,\\\\\n   100\t \\phi_j^{(b)}&=\\int_0^\\infty\\!\\frac{\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\left[e^{i\\omega a}(e^{i\\omega u_j}-1)\n   101\t \\int_0^1\\prod_{k\\ne j}(1-t+t e^{i\\omega u_k})\\,dt\\right]}\n   102\t {\\sinh(\\pi\\omega)}\\,d\\omega. \\label{eq:logistic}\n   103\t\\end{align}\n   104\tThe inner polynomial has degree at most $d-1$, so $\\lceil d/2\\rceil$ Gauss--Legendre nodes integrate it exactly in exact arithmetic. Prefix/suffix products avoid division by possibly zero factors. The outer frequency integral is numerical, not an exact logistic SHAP solver.\n   105\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L12-L40]]\n   106\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L97-L140]]\n   107\t\n   108\tThe implementation truncates frequencies at 8, uses eight-node quadrature panels, and refines up to 2048 frequency nodes. It requires two consecutive mesh agreements and an endpoint residual within $10^{-8}$, with an analytic tail tolerance of $10^{-10}$. Mesh agreement does not bound every coordinate's discretization error. If any background row fails numerical checks, the whole logistic integration is rejected and the sampled fallback is retained. All supplied background rows are averaged; these checks do not select a favorable subset.\n   109\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L43-L94]]\n   110\t\n   111\t\\paragraph{Dispatch and sampled fallback.}\n   112\tForest structure is used directly. Other games are enumerated when the cap covers their nontrivial coalitions; otherwise logistic integration is considered. The fallback combines independent complete permutation paths with complement-paired kernel-distributed masks and an importance-corrected, endpoint-constrained regression. An internal signed top-feature separation test may select the path estimate instead. We have no recorded dispatch frequencies or test-acceptance coverage and make no empirical certification claim. The source ordering matters: high-cap exhaustive behavior must not be attributed to quadrature alone.\n   113\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L163-L197]]\n   114\t\n   115\t\\subsection{Design Rationale and Component Comparisons}\n   116\tThe design removes sampling where model structure permits same-game algebra, rather than changing what absent features mean. Forest leaf constraints expose a finite conjunction game; logistic additivity in the logit allows a probability-scale transform. We therefore expect the largest benefits where prediction-only estimates are inaccurate under the supplied cap. Deterministic computation is not itself the hypothesis test: an incorrect deterministic vector would be perfectly repeatable.\n   117\t\n   118\tTwo matched development comparisons examine the forest component with the nonforest computation retained, and logistic integration with the forest component retained. Readable source differences support this limited interpretation. These are narrower comparisons than a claim that every observed configuration difference isolates a single mathematical choice: the components also change access to model structure and computational work. There is no factorial study of quadrature safeguards, background size, or permutation-test acceptance.\n   119\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/sampled/reliability.py]]\n   120\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/forest/reliability.py]]\n   121\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py]]\n   122\t\n   123\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n   124\t\\paragraph{Data and frozen models.}\n   125\tThe final evaluation uses German Credit and ACSIncome-2024-RELSHIPP, each with logistic regression and a random forest. German retains 300 training, 100 validation, and 100 explanation records with 20 feature groups. ACS retains 20000, 6667, and 6667 records with 10 groups. Only the first 16 explanation records and first 16 training rows are used as queries and background in each condition; these are fixed across repeated calls, not randomly redrawn per explanation. Thus the split sizes should not be mistaken for the number of explained individuals.\n   126\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   127\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L687-L718]]\n   128\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py#L61-L155]]\n   129\t\n   130\tGerman records are partitioned by normalized predictor content into disjoint development and final subsets. ACS uses California for development and New York for final evaluation, after excluding cross-state predictor-content overlaps. The final ACS task replaces the historical \\texttt{RELP} field, absent from the retained release schema, with native \\texttt{RELSHIPP}; it is not bit-identical to historical ACSIncome. Other predictors, the adult population filter, and the income threshold are retained in the acquisition code. These choices define the evaluated population, not a population-representative survey analysis.\n   131\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/data_provenance.json]]\n   132\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py#L87-L155]]\n   133\t\n   134\tCategorical vocabularies are fitted on training data and encoded ordinally; numerical missing values use training medians. Training means and standard deviations standardize all supplied groups. No one-hot masking study is included. Logistic models use \\texttt{lbfgs}, a 500-iteration cap and tolerance $10^{-7}$. Forests use 64 trees, maximum depth 6, and a single training thread. Model parameters and preprocessing remain frozen within a repeat comparison. Ordinal categories can impose artificial order; our results concern these fitted predictors, not optimal credit or income models.\n   135\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L687-L718]]\n   136\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L817-L848]]\n   137\t\n   138\t\\paragraph{Caps, seeds, and references.}\n   139\tFinal caps are 64, 256, and 1024. Each condition has six repeated structure-aware calls per cap for seeds 104729, 130363, and 155921. Each seed produces a fitted model/reference evaluation; queries and backgrounds are reused. ACS references enumerate all coalitions of the ten-feature game. German references average two independent library estimates, each with cap 4096. Their coordinatewise half-difference is retained as a reference-disagreement proxy, not a confidence interval or an error bound for their mean.\n   140\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   141\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L848-L858]]\n   142\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1314-L1320]]\n   143\t\n   144\t\\paragraph{Reporting and uncertainty.}\n   145\tWe average metrics over query instances within each condition, then over the recorded seeds with equal weights. Repeated calls on the same query are not independent individuals. The stored 95\\% intervals resample the 16 query-level mean agreements using 1000 bootstrap resamples, after averaging caps and repeats within each fitted condition. They are neither pairwise-method confidence intervals nor uncertainty over retraining, background selection, or future deployment. Main plots show seed-level points and means, not invented standard errors. Library comparators have one recorded estimate per seed and cap; their within-model repeated-call consistency was not measured by this final protocol.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
