{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '150,245p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,85p' references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/wall_seconds]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Main Fixed-Game Comparison}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:final} compares computations within each dataset/model condition. At cap 64, mean structure-aware agreement is 0.9966 for German logistic and 0.9937 for German forest, against 0.9347 and 0.8806 for the one-shot library baseline. On ACS, both structure-aware conditions round to 1.0000 against the exhaustive reference. The corresponding one-shot library means are 0.9577 and 0.9636. These comparisons support improved numerical agreement in the retained low-cap settings, not a claim about new people, backgrounds, or classifiers.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/final_reference_comparison.pdf}\n\\caption{Final reference agreement by dataset, classifier, and coalition cap. Dots show the three fitted-condition seeds; lines connect cap-wise means, not a temporal trajectory. German references average two high-cap estimates; ACS references are exhaustive. The common zero-to-one scale makes the severe split-cap German failure visible; Table~\\ref{tab:main} resolves near-ceiling differences.}\n\\label{fig:final}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Final cap-64 mean reference agreement and structure-aware reference sign agreement, averaged over seeds. The top-three reference overlap is 1 throughout for the structure-aware computation. Values rounded to 1.0000 are not claims of exact real-arithmetic equality.}\n\\label{tab:main}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCondition & One-shot & Split cap & Structure & Signs\\\\\n\\midrule\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\nGerman / logistic & 0.9347 & 0.3341 & 0.9966 & 1.0000 \\\\\nGerman / forest & 0.8806 & 0.2995 & 0.9937 & 0.9979 \\\\\nACS / logistic & 0.9577 & 0.9349 & 1.0000 & 1.0000 \\\\\nACS / forest & 0.9636 & 0.9378 & 1.0000 & 1.0000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n\\end{table}\n\nIncreasing the library cap improves mean reference agreement in all four final conditions. At cap 1024, its German logistic and forest means reach 0.9889 and 0.9802, while both ACS means round to 1.0000. The implemented exhaustive dispatch can also apply at this ACS cap. The evidence therefore does not support saying that structure-aware computation is necessary for high agreement in every measured setting. Nor does an unchanged structure-aware curve establish budget efficiency: parameter access allows computations that are not charged as coalition predictions.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L173-L184]]\n\nThe recorded aggregate normalized reference agreement is 0.9975639. We do not use the evaluator's mixed sign summary as if it were pure reference agreement: directly pooling the recorded reference-comparison cells gives sign agreement 0.9994792. Every structure-aware top-three set agrees with its reference, yet near-perfect membership and signs do not imply identical attribution magnitudes. Supplementary tables preserve the condition- and seed-level evidence instead of collapsing reference quality into a single success label.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/reference_fidelity]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/reference_sign_mean]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Repeat Consistency Is Stronger Than Observed Reference Equality}\nAll retained structure-aware repeat-pair top-three and sign agreements equal 1. Inspecting the stored vectors also yields a maximum coordinate range across repeats of zero. This is consistent with deterministic dispatch in these conditions, but dispatch counts are not recorded. It does not show that an arbitrary fallback call will be deterministic or accurately attributed. There are only six calls per cap on a small fixed query set, and no population-level zero-failure guarantee follows.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/max_repeat_vector_difference]]\n\nGerman provides a useful separation: identical repeated outputs still differ from the approximate reference. The recorded query-bootstrap interval for German logistic at seed 104729 is $[0.99618,0.99709]$; the analogous forest interval is $[0.99292,0.99442]$. These intervals describe query heterogeneity conditional on the recorded reference, not uncertainty about the true Shapley vector. The mean absolute coordinate half-differences between German reference replicates are approximately $1.05\\times10^{-4}$ for logistic and $1.20\\times10^{-4}$ for forests. We cannot attribute all residual estimate-to-reference distance to either side from that proxy alone.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/observations/fidelity_instance_bootstrap_ci95]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/1/observations/fidelity_instance_bootstrap_ci95]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/reference_uncertainty]]\n\nACS has a stronger computational interpretation because its reference exhausts the specified finite game. Agreement there supports numerical fidelity to that game, without validating background choice or impossible hybrid records. The observed endpoint residual averages $1.28\\times10^{-11}$; recorded control pass rate is 1, failed-call fraction is zero, and replay error is zero. These are narrow numerical and operational checks, not independent evidence that feature attributions are good reasons for decisions.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/efficiency_error]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_pass_rate]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/failed_fraction]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/replay_error]]\n\n\\subsection{Budget Splitting and Component Sensitivity}\nAveraging is not automatically helpful under a fixed total cap. At cap 64 the split-cap German logistic mean is 0.3341 and the forest mean is 0.2995, substantially below the one-shot means. By cap 256 those split-cap means recover to 0.9699 and 0.9448. The result establishes a failure of this particular low-cap split, not a general failure of averaging independent estimators. The retained diagnostics do not identify a unique numerical cause; reduced fit information is a plausible interpretation, not a measured mechanism.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/component_comparison.pdf}\n\\caption{Matched component comparisons on development data only. Left: forest computation with and without the exact leaf-game component. Right: logistic computation with and without integration. Each category shows the two recorded seed values and a mean tick; the vertical axis is restricted to $[0.8,1]$. Ger/ACS denote dataset and labels underneath give coalition caps. The unchanged model family is retained in each comparison; structural access and work still differ.}\n\\label{fig:components}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nDevelopment comparisons in Figure~\\ref{fig:components} support family-localized changes. At cap 64, German forest reference agreement changes from 0.858--0.870 without the leaf-game component to 0.9924--0.9929 with it. German logistic changes from 0.946--0.951 without integration to approximately 0.9963 with integration. In both ACS comparisons, the structure-aware outputs agree with exhaustive references to the displayed precision. These are matched configuration comparisons, not evidence that an untested safeguard or formula alone caused every gain.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/development]]\n\nThe sampled development estimates also show nonmonotone cap behavior: ACS forest mean agreement without its structure component falls from approximately 0.9674 at cap 64 to 0.9512 at cap 256. German logistic without integration similarly falls from about 0.9488 to 0.9137. This concerns the custom sampled fallback, not the final library curve. More sampled coalitions need not improve an individual finite estimate, and the recorded comparisons do not isolate whether sampling mix, fit conditioning, or another interaction explains the pattern. No missing ablation is treated as a measured failure.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/development]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What a single explanation supports.}\nFor these retained games, a single structure-aware output is empirically representative of its repeated-call outputs and closely matches the available reference. This is a statement about computation. A deployment policy still needs a tolerance on magnitude error, ranking errors, and sign errors that reflects its use case. We did not evaluate such a policy, elicited human requirements, or a threshold determining when a vector is sufficiently reliable to justify a decision. In particular, high mean agreement can hide rare query-specific behavior and the normalization in Equation~\\ref{eq:agreement} does not directly encode decision harm.\n\n\\paragraph{Reference and dependence limits.}\nThe German reference's own approximation prevents assigning all remaining discrepancy to the tested method. Exhaustive ACS attribution removes that specific ambiguity, not the choice of game. The small fixed background, ordinal feature representation, and replacement of missing features can create unrealistic hybrids. There is no conditional-game, background-shift, refitting-stability, adversarial, or user study. Fixed queries are reused across seeds, so pooled cells and the many repeat pairs do not create a large independent sample. Bootstrap intervals do not account for shared reference bias.\n\n\\paragraph{Access, selection, and external validity.}\nStructure-aware computation uses more information than a prediction-only service may expose. We lack candidate-only timing, branch counts, and equal-time comparisons. It is therefore inappropriate to translate the cap-wise agreement advantage into an unrestricted speed or cost advantage. Development selection introduces adaptivity; the held-out evaluation narrows but does not eliminate all selection concerns. The source protocol supplies only two tabular datasets and two model families, with few queries. Geographic separation for ACS is not a claim of cross-task generalization to medicine, other years, or neural models. Source inspection supports the released computational mechanisms, while unexecuted independent validation scripts are not counted as additional experimental evidence.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope of the contribution.}\nThe strongest supported finding is conditional: structural access can produce repeatable, reference-consistent computations of the chosen finite game under the supplied caps. This does not establish universal reliability of one-shot SHAP, superiority over unmeasured estimators, or validity of its use in high-risk decisions. The negative split-cap result and the unresolved German reference discrepancy are part of that finding, not exceptions to remove from the report.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nComputational reliability of a feature attribution should distinguish repeating an answer from computing the intended answer. For the frozen German Credit and adapted ACS income models studied here, forest leaf-game attribution and probability-scale logistic integration yield identical observed repeated-call vectors and high agreement with their respective references. The library baseline approaches exhaustive ACS agreement at the largest cap, while dividing a small cap between two estimates can be substantially worse than one library call. Thus neither repeated execution nor averaging supplies a universal remedy.\n\n@misc{Lundberg2017,\n  title = {{A Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions}},\n  author = {Scott Lundberg and Su-In Lee},\n  year = {2017},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1705.07874},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.1705.07874}\n}\n\n@misc{Krishna2022,\n  title = {{The Disagreement Problem in Explainable Machine Learning: A Practitioner's Perspective}},\n  author = {Satyapriya Krishna and Tessa Han and Alex Gu and Shahin Jabbari and Zhiwei Steven Wu and Himabindu Lakkaraju},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.01602},\n  doi = {10.21203/rs.3.rs-2963888/v1}\n}\n\n@misc{Yuan2022,\n  title = {{An empirical study of the effect of background data size on the stability of SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) for deep learning models}},\n  author = {Han Yuan and Mingxuan Liu and Lican Kang and Chenkui Miao and Ying Nian Wu and et al and Michael O. Krauthammer},\n  year = {2022},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2204.11351},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2204.11351}\n}\n\n@misc{Musco2024,\n  title = {{Provably Accurate Shapley Value Estimation via Leverage Score Sampling}},\n  author = {Christopher Musco and R. Teal Witter},\n  year = {2024},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.01917},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2410.01917}\n}\n\n@misc{Chen2020,\n  title = {{True to the Model or True to the Data?}},\n  author = {Hugh Chen and Joseph D. Janizek and Scott Lundberg and Su-In Lee},\n  year = {2020},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.16234},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2006.16234}\n}\n\n@misc{Marx2020,\n  title = {{Predictive Multiplicity in Classification}},\n  author = {Charles T. Marx and Flavio P. Calmon and Berk Ustun},\n  year = {2019},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1909.06677},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.1909.06677}\n}\n\n@misc{Nogueira2018,\n  title = {{On the stability of feature selection algorithms}},\n  author = {Sarah Nogueira and Konstantinos Sechidis and Gavin Brown B},\n  year = {2018},\n  howpublished = {Journal of Machine Learning Research, 18(174):1--54},\n  doi = {10.5555/3122009.3242031}\n}\n\n@misc{Webber2010,\n  title = {{A similarity measure for indefinite rankings}},\n  author = {William Webber and Alistair Moffat and Justin Zobel},\n  year = {2010},\n  howpublished = {ACM Transactions on Information Systems},\n  doi = {10.1145/1852102.1852106}\n}\n\n@misc{Ding2021,\n  title = {{Retiring Adult: New Datasets for Fair Machine Learning}},\n  author = {Frances Ding and Moritz Hardt and John Arthur Miller and Ludwig Schmidt},\n  year = {2021},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2108.04884},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2108.04884}\n}\n\n@misc{Gorishniy2021,\n  title = {{Revisiting Deep Learning Models for Tabular Data}},\n  author = {Yury Gorishniy and Ivan Rubachev and Valentin Khrulkov and Artem Babenko},\n  year = {2021},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.11959},\n  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2106.11959}\n}\n\n@misc{Lundberg2018,\n  title = {{Consistent Individualized Feature Attribution for Tree Ensembles}},\n  author = {Scott Lundberg and Gabriel Erion and Su-In Lee},\n  year = {2018},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1802.03888},\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
