{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '207,255p' paper.tex; sed -n '256,325p' paper.tex; head -45 claims_grounding.json; head -35 references_provenance.json; head -45 paper_strategy.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '207,255p' paper.tex; sed -n '256,325p' paper.tex; head -45 claims_grounding.json; head -35 references_provenance.json; head -45 paper_strategy.json\"","aggregated_output":"% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/efficiency_error]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_pass_rate]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/failed_fraction]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/replay_error]]\n\n\\subsection{Budget Splitting and Component Sensitivity}\nAveraging is not automatically helpful under a fixed total cap. At cap 64 the split-cap German logistic mean is 0.3341 and the forest mean is 0.2995, substantially below the one-shot means. By cap 256 those split-cap means recover to 0.9699 and 0.9448. The result establishes a failure of this particular low-cap split, not a general failure of averaging independent estimators. The retained diagnostics do not identify a unique numerical cause; reduced fit information is a plausible interpretation, not a measured mechanism.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/component_comparison.pdf}\n\\caption{Matched component comparisons on development data only. Left: forest computation with and without the exact leaf-game component. Right: logistic computation with and without integration. Each category shows the two recorded seed values and a mean tick; the vertical axis is restricted to $[0.8,1]$. Ger/ACS denote dataset and labels underneath give coalition caps. The unchanged model family is retained in each comparison; structural access and work still differ.}\n\\label{fig:components}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nDevelopment comparisons in Figure~\\ref{fig:components} support family-localized changes. At cap 64, German forest reference agreement changes from 0.858--0.870 without the leaf-game component to 0.9924--0.9929 with it. German logistic changes from 0.946--0.951 without integration to approximately 0.9963 with integration. In both ACS comparisons, the structure-aware outputs agree with exhaustive references to the displayed precision. These are matched configuration comparisons, not evidence that an untested safeguard or formula alone caused every gain.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/development]]\n\nThe sampled development estimates also show nonmonotone cap behavior: ACS forest mean agreement without its structure component falls from approximately 0.9674 at cap 64 to 0.9512 at cap 256. German logistic without integration similarly falls from about 0.9488 to 0.9137. This concerns the custom sampled fallback, not the final library curve. More sampled coalitions need not improve an individual finite estimate, and the recorded comparisons do not isolate whether sampling mix, fit conditioning, or another interaction explains the pattern. No missing ablation is treated as a measured failure.\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/development]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What a single explanation supports.}\nFor these retained games, a single structure-aware output is empirically representative of its repeated-call outputs and closely matches the available reference. This is a statement about computation. A deployment policy still needs a tolerance on magnitude error, ranking errors, and sign errors that reflects its use case. We did not evaluate such a policy, elicited human requirements, or a threshold determining when a vector is sufficiently reliable to justify a decision. In particular, high mean agreement can hide rare query-specific behavior and the normalization in Equation~\\ref{eq:agreement} does not directly encode decision harm.\n\n\\paragraph{Reference and dependence limits.}\nThe German reference's own approximation prevents assigning all remaining discrepancy to the tested method. Exhaustive ACS attribution removes that specific ambiguity, not the choice of game. The small fixed background, ordinal feature representation, and replacement of missing features can create unrealistic hybrids. There is no conditional-game, background-shift, refitting-stability, adversarial, or user study. Fixed queries are reused across seeds, so pooled cells and the many repeat pairs do not create a large independent sample. Bootstrap intervals do not account for shared reference bias.\n\n\\paragraph{Access, selection, and external validity.}\nStructure-aware computation uses more information than a prediction-only service may expose. We lack candidate-only timing, branch counts, and equal-time comparisons. It is therefore inappropriate to translate the cap-wise agreement advantage into an unrestricted speed or cost advantage. Development selection introduces adaptivity; the held-out evaluation narrows but does not eliminate all selection concerns. The source protocol supplies only two tabular datasets and two model families, with few queries. Geographic separation for ACS is not a claim of cross-task generalization to medicine, other years, or neural models. Source inspection supports the released computational mechanisms, while unexecuted independent validation scripts are not counted as additional experimental evidence.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope of the contribution.}\nThe strongest supported finding is conditional: structural access can produce repeatable, reference-consistent computations of the chosen finite game under the supplied caps. This does not establish universal reliability of one-shot SHAP, superiority over unmeasured estimators, or validity of its use in high-risk decisions. The negative split-cap result and the unresolved German reference discrepancy are part of that finding, not exceptions to remove from the report.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nComputational reliability of a feature attribution should distinguish repeating an answer from computing the intended answer. For the frozen German Credit and adapted ACS income models studied here, forest leaf-game attribution and probability-scale logistic integration yield identical observed repeated-call vectors and high agreement with their respective references. The library baseline approaches exhaustive ACS agreement at the largest cap, while dividing a small cap between two estimates can be substantially worse than one library call. Thus neither repeated execution nor averaging supplies a universal remedy.\n\nThe strength of the conclusion depends on what is held fixed and what is known. Exhaustive finite-game references support a sharper computational interpretation than approximate references. Structural access, fixed backgrounds, and a small reused query set prevent equal-time or broad deployment claims. A reliable numerical solution is one prerequisite for using an attribution, not evidence that the background, feature semantics, classifier, or downstream decision is justified. Future assessments need application-specific tolerances, broader query coverage, reference diagnostics, and matched cost accounting before making that stronger claim.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Fixed-Game Construction, Data Provenance, and Models}\nThe public acquisition and split implementation is included as\n\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluation/capture.py}; data URLs, release digests, licenses, and the schema adapter are in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluation/data\\_provenance.json}. German Credit originates from UCI DOI 10.24432/C5NC77 under CC BY 4.0. ACS records originate from the 2024 one-year California and New York PUMS releases. The folktables code is MIT-licensed; the Census materials are public government data with site terms archived by the original acquisition. No separate SPDX data license is inferred.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/data_provenance.json]]\n\nThe acquisition hashes normalized raw predictor content before population filtering, splitting, or preprocessing. Numeric fields use canonical finite decimal strings, categorical fields use Unicode normalization and normalized whitespace, and missing values have a typed sentinel. Predictor hashes exclude identifiers and labels. German content groups are split between development and final data, then deterministically partitioned into training, validation, and explanation subsets. All California/New York predictor-content overlaps are removed before ACS content groups are selected. Such exclusion changes population composition and does not substitute for population weighting.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py#L41-L155]]\n\nACS uses \\texttt{AGEP, COW, SCHL, MAR, OCCP, POBP, RELSHIPP, WKHP, SEX, RAC1P}. The acquisition retains the adult income population restriction and the binary \\texttt{PINCP>50000} target. Native \\texttt{RELSHIPP} is used without inventing a crosswalk to the absent \\texttt{RELP}. German positive labels correspond to the original code 2. These target conventions matter when interpreting attribution signs; a positive value raises the serialized model's chosen positive-class probability, not an abstract notion of benefit.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py#L72-L151]]\n\nTraining vocabularies map sorted categorical values to integer codes, with unseen values mapped to $-1$. Numeric imputation and all scaling statistics are training-fitted. The serialized predictor is checked against the fitted estimator on queries and background before explanation. This is a finite probe check, not proof of equivalent behavior on every hybrid input. Final validation accuracies are 0.67 for German logistic, 0.65--0.69 for German forests, approximately 0.7609 for ACS logistic and 0.7755--0.7773 for ACS forests. Logistic fits report convergence with 19 iterations on German and 13 on ACS. These diagnostics characterize the explained predictors; the attribution study does not establish predictive adequacy.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L687-L718]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L838-L847]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Structure-Aware Computation and Numerical Safeguards}\n\\paragraph{Leaf contributions.}\nFor a viable leaf, the expected indicator under independent feature inclusion with probability $t$ is $t^p(1-t)^q$. Including a required-positive feature changes this by $t^{p-1}(1-t)^q$, while including a required-negative feature changes it by $-t^p(1-t)^{q-1}$. Integrating each marginal from zero to one yields Equation~\\ref{eq:leaf}. Features not constrained by that leaf contribute zero. A constant leaf contributes no feature attribution, though it contributes to the empty/full value. Repeated splits are intersected into lower-exclusive and upper-inclusive bounds; contradictory paths are removed. The exact finite-game claim depends on matching these traversal semantics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/forest.py#L11-L81]]\n\n\\paragraph{Frequency truncation is not discretization certification.}\nIn Equation~\\ref{eq:logistic}, the phase difference has magnitude at most two and every inclusion factor has magnitude at most one. Thus the omitted tail beyond frequency $W$ is bounded coordinatewise by\n\\begin{equation}\n 2\\int_W^\\infty\\frac{d\\omega}{\\sinh(\\pi\\omega)}\n =-\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\log\\tanh\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi W}{2}\\right).\n\\end{equation}\nThis is the source implementation's frequency-tail bound. It does not bound mesh discretization error on the retained interval. The implementation uses agreement under refinement and endpoint conservation as additional numerical diagnostics, rejects nonfinite arithmetic or excessive phase ranges, and averages all background rows only after successful integration. Prefix/suffix products keep zero mixture factors well-defined. The wrapper catches floating-point overflow and invalid arithmetic and returns rejection without consuming the sampling generator.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L12-L140]]\n\n\\paragraph{Fallback safeguards.}\nIndependent permutation paths provide bounded marginal observations in $[-1,1]$. For $m$ complete paths, coordinate sample variance $s_j^2$, and $\\alpha=0.05$, the implemented simultaneous radius is\n\\begin{equation}\n r_j=\\sqrt{\\frac{2s_j^2\\log(4d/\\alpha)}{m}}\n       +\\frac{14\\log(4d/\\alpha)}{3(m-1)}.\n\\end{equation}\nIt is used only when at least two complete paths exist. The path estimate is selected only when absolute-value intervals strictly separate selected from excluded features and the selected signs exclude zero. Otherwise regression is retained. Even if the underlying bound's conditions hold, this is not a guarantee conditional on selection and supplies no observed certification rate here. Permutation paths, not individual within-path marginals, are independent sampling units. Endpoint-constrained regression is not covered by that path-bound calculation.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L66-L161]]\n\n\\paragraph{Implementation evidence.}\nReadable files in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/selected/} contain dispatch, integration, forest calculation, and the attribution-only response interface. The corresponding development snapshots are in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/sampled/} and \\texttt{source\\_evidence/forest/}. The release includes independent small-tree and logistic validation routines, but there is no recorded execution of those routines in the retained research evidence. They are not additional controls, replications, or measured ablations. Provenance hashes identify artifacts; the equations above are supported by the readable source, not merely by a prior audit verdict.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/validate_forest.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/validate_logistic_fourier.py]]\n\nConcrete source anchors are \\path{source_evidence/selected/forest.py:11} for path intersections and leaf contributions,\n\\path{source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py:12} for the numerical integrand and refinements,\nand \\path{source_evidence/selected/reliability.py:163} for dispatch. Metric definitions are in\n\\path{source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py:1014}.\n\n\\subsection{Episode-Level Reliability and Reference Uncertainty}\nTable~\\ref{tab:extended} lists every final non-replay condition and cap. Means within each row average the fixed query set and, for structure-aware outputs, repeated calls. No new evaluation is run to create this table. Table~\\ref{tab:intervals} preserves the stored query-bootstrap intervals, averaged over caps and repeated calls as specified in the main text. They must not be read as uncertainty for the difference between two methods.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\begin{longtable}{llrrrr}\n\\caption{Final seed/condition/cap reference agreement. Display precision can hide residual error near one.}\\label{tab:extended}\\\\\n\\toprule Seed & Condition & Cap & One-shot & Split cap & Structure\\\\\\midrule\n\\endfirsthead\n\\toprule Seed & Condition & Cap & One-shot & Split cap & Structure\\\\\\midrule\n\\endhead\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n104729 & German / logistic & 64 & 0.9292 & 0.3134 & 0.9967 \\\\\n104729 & German / logistic & 256 & 0.9754 & 0.9713 & 0.9967 \\\\\n104729 & German / logistic & 1024 & 0.9894 & 0.9893 & 0.9967 \\\\\n104729 & German / forest & 64 & 0.8836 & 0.2904 & 0.9937 \\\\\n104729 & German / forest & 256 & 0.9547 & 0.9454 & 0.9937 \\\\\n104729 & German / forest & 1024 & 0.9806 & 0.9777 & 0.9937 \\\\\n104729 & ACS / logistic & 64 & 0.9604 & 0.9363 & 1.0000 \\\\\n104729 & ACS / logistic & 256 & 0.9834 & 0.9804 & 1.0000 \\\\\n104729 & ACS / logistic & 1024 & 1.0000 & 0.9936 & 1.0000 \\\\\n104729 & ACS / forest & 64 & 0.9604 & 0.9348 & 1.0000 \\\\\n104729 & ACS / forest & 256 & 0.9853 & 0.9843 & 1.0000 \\\\\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Final qualification independently reports completed/qualified/true. This concerns documented integrity, coverage, execution and controls, not deployment validity.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.qualification.status\",\n        \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n        \"final.qualification.correct\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\n        \"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1253-L1273\"\n      ],\n      \"scope\": \"final fixed-game evaluation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Aggregate final normalized reference agreement is 0.9975639445869278, rounded to 0.99756 in the abstract.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.reference_fidelity\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"scope\": \"final fixed-game evaluation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"All final structure-aware reference top-three overlaps and repeat-pair top-three/sign agreements equal one; these are separate assessments.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.top3_overlap\",\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"scope\": \"final fixed-game evaluation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Stored vectors have zero maximum coordinate range across repeats within each fixed cap/model/query.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\n{\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-07T04:18:04.468977+00:00\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.1705.07874\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.2202.01602\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.2204.11351\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.2410.01917\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.2006.16234\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.1909.06677\",\n    \"title:On the Stability of Feature Selection Algorithms\",\n    \"10.1145/1852102.1852106\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.2108.04884\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.2106.11959\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.1802.03888\",\n    \"10.1287/mnsc.18.5.64\",\n    \"10.1515/9781400881970-018\",\n    \"10.1016/j.cor.2008.04.004\",\n    \"10.1080/01621459.1963.10500830\",\n    \"10.48550/arXiv.0907.3740\",\n    \"10.1214/aos/1176344552\",\n    \"10.1023/A:1010933404324\",\n    \"10.1002/9781118548387\",\n    \"title:Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python\",\n    \"10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2\",\n    \"10.1038/s41592-019-0686-2\",\n    \"10.1109/MCSE.2007.55\",\n    \"10.25080/Majora-92bf1922-00a\",\n    \"10.24432/C5NC77\"\n  ],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.openalex.W2618851150\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://openalex.org/W2618851150\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"Lundberg2017\",\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Fixed-Game SHAP Reliability: Structure, Budgets, and Reference Limits\",\n  \"central_question\": \"For frozen tabular classifiers and a fixed empirical probability-scale Shapley game, how do sampled and structure-aware computations compare in single-call reference agreement and repeated-call consistency under finite coalition caps, and what reliability claims remain unsupported?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the evaluated German Credit and ACS income conditions, structure-aware forest attribution and probability-scale logistic integration yield perfectly consistent observed repeated-call top-three sets and signs and high reference agreement, but reference uncertainty, restricted game semantics, and unequal computational access prevent interpreting these results as general deployment reliability or equal-time superiority.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"A bounded computational-reliability study is supported by final per-instance attribution arrays, repeated-call pairs, library comparators, controls, and uncertainty summaries. Four dataset/model conditions test one shared fixed-game thesis, with forest algebra and logistic integration as condition-specific computations. The final evaluation is independently completed and qualified; this is a documented evaluation outcome, not scientific proof of general reliability. A certificate-centered paper is weaker because certificate decisions and acceptance coverage are not recorded. A general high-risk deployment paper exceeds the fixed-background, scalar-encoded, small-instance evidence. A logistic-only paper discards the independently useful forest conditions. A search-success narrative confuses development evidence with the final evaluation. The chosen story retains direct comparisons and limitations without requiring new experiments.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"recentered\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"No edit-mode files, previous_paper directory, or prior manuscript were supplied. Recentered refers to narrowing the broad source-task motivation to the evaluated fixed-game computational question, not to revising an unseen manuscript. Preserving the broad deployment story was rejected; a shared cross-model computation story was selected over a logistic-only recentering.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"There are no staged prior/new manuscript pairs or extra_tasks/aggregated directories; no inherited title, thesis, or figure plan is assumed.\",\n    \"paper_data.json.final_evaluation supplies three final episodes and four public-data/model conditions with one-shot library and split-budget two-start comparators; these observations, not qualitative memory, determine the central results.\",\n    \"paper_data.json.downstream_study and study.* grounding sources are absent. Do not invent a frozen-study selection contract, search-score differences, attempt counts, or an original-incumbent comparison from memory.\",\n    \"Recoverable source under results/ideas/03_probability_scale_logistic_shap_by_bounded_fourier_quadrature/program_1/src implements both forest and logistic computations. Earlier reliability.py files differ by the corresponding imports/dispatches, supporting bounded development-set component comparisons rather than a chronology.\",\n    \"The best-program manifest links the recoverable implementation to the final-tested configuration at path/selection level. The final manifest records a program-tree digest, but this strategy does not independently establish a byte-for-byte final snapshot match; algorithm descriptions are source-grounded and measured behavior is final-evidence-grounded.\",\n    \"VENUE.md and the actual ICLR style require a single-column, anonymized, 8–9 body-page paper; the plan uses readable full-text-width figures rather than assuming a two-column format.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"One-shot library Kernel SHAP\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The evaluator records identity-link shap.KernelExplainer outputs, l1_reg=0.0, for the same frozen model, queries, empirical background, and nominal coalition caps. This is a measured comparator, not a literature-only baseline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Two-start Kernel SHAP with split coalition caps\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Two independent library estimates each receive half the nominal cap and are averaged. This tests a practical repeat-and-average alternative without silently doubling its coalition allocation; actual row counts and endpoint overhead need not match the one-shot method.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Structure-aware fixed-game computation\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"One shared estimator family preserves the finite empirical interventional probability game: exact leaf-literal algebra for forests, bounded Fourier/Owen integration for nonexhaustive logistic requests, exhaustive nonforest computation when the cap covers all subsets, and the existing sampled fallback on numerical rejection. Do not claim observed branch frequencies from unrecorded internal diagnostics.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Sampled path/regression configuration\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The configuration without either structure-aware component has measured development-set outputs. Compare it directly with the forest-enabled configuration on forest conditions; it is not the untouched original incumbent and is not a separately final-evaluated method. Its internal confidence-bound code does not supply measured certificate coverage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Forest-enabled, sampled-logistic configuration\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Its source retains the exact forest branch but lacks logistic quadrature. Matched development episodes isolate the logistic dispatch addition; unchanged forest outputs act as a component-locality check. These comparisons are descriptive selected-development evidence, not independent confirmation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Same-game references and analytic controls\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
